A||||5||85ZainoGT||HELP!!!! WINDOW TINT IS PEELING ALREADY||||1||
Z||000000||85ZainoGT||10-05-2001||04:29 PM||||I tinted my windows monday night and I rolled them down for the first time thursday afternoon, now today I get out about 10 minutes ago(3:28) and my tint is starting to peel from the top?  anything I can do about this?<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||10-05-2001||04:38 PM||||Squirt more water on them and squiggie the air out, don't lower windows for 2 weeks. One of three things happened, you didn't apply them correctly,  there has been to much humidity in your area for them to dry properly, or the tint film was sub-standard.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroFanaticus2||10-05-2001||05:20 PM||||If it's peeling from the top the the film was either not placed properly and a little bit of them film was above the rounded cut on the top of the window, or it did not have ample time to dry, or as the post above said, it may be the conditions in your area - cold or humidity, etc.<BR>Try this...<BR>Squirt a little of the application fliud into the area where it is peeling, take a hair drier or hot air gun and blow hot air on the window from the OUTSIDE while squeegeeing the film back down until it holds it's place.<BR>Then move the hair drier around the complete outer edge of the window to make sure it is completely dry all the way around.<BR>Then, very carefully take a single edge razor blade and from the INSIDE of the window hold it at about a 45 degree angle against the top of the window, but angled so it will trim below the 'curve' along the top of the window. Carefully slice your way along the entire edge of the window - trimming off any film that extends beyond where it should be.  Hold the razor at a steady angle and don't go too fast, make sure you CUT the film and aren't TEARING it.  Hang on a few minutes and I'll post a picture of what I am talking about.<p>[This message has been edited by FieroFanaticus2 (edited 10-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroFanaticus2||10-05-2001||05:43 PM||||OK.<BR>This pic shows the outer edge of the window, marked by the pinkish color.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PA050004.JPG"> <P><BR>Holding the razor at an angle to trim around the outer edge and below the 'curve'.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PA050006.JPG"> <P>There should not be any film that is on or above the 'curve'.  1/8 inch or so is needed to keep it from peeling.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PA050007.JPG"> <P>Hope these help you some.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JSocha||10-05-2001||06:09 PM||||More then likely, it caught a weak spot while curing.<P>Also, did you use soapy water?  I never use soapy water when I tint, just lots and lots of water while I work with it.  Comes out better in the long run and you don't get discolored spots.<P>1) FieroFanaticus2 hit it on the head regarding an 1/8" expansion/contraction releif from the edge of the window to the edge of your tint.  You can get less, but you need to have practice.<P>2) If you need to trim your tint with a razor, use a thin metal straight edge (or thin metal ruler) and do small length cuts and then repostion your straight edge.  You will get better results then free handing it.<P>3)  Wet it where it is lifting and squeegy it like previously mentioned.  You may have problems though if the backing is now contaminated with dust or skin oils.<P>4)  ALLOW A FEW DAYS TO CURE before operating the window and/or opening and closing the door!  The moisture is evaporating over time underneath, but is going through and semi, yet impermable membrane.<P>5)  After a day or two, use some CLEAR FINGER NAIL Polish/paint to seal the edge all the way around.  This way it lessens the chance of it snagging a weak spot and subsequently peeling.  <P>I have gotten a 1/16" border all the way around doing this on most of the tint jobs I have done and there still lasting while people who have it done professionally have replaced their tint already due to peeling.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||86seyellow||10-06-2001||07:04 AM||||Well.<BR>Like I said I did all 3 of my winodws the darkest I could get them in about an hour. I used to do tinting when I was younger. HEre is the trick. On all the questionable edges apply clear nail polish, Yes clear nail polish. ALl the pros do this to ensure a tight fitting at edges or when they have to redo their work<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/86seyellow_new1.JPG">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieropix1_copy1.jpg"> <BR>I can go as fast as I want unless I get caught||||reg||1
